    {
      "type": "post",
      "id": "7468262826755653632",
      "entityId": "7468262826755653632",
      "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/woodsrach_anything-worth-doing-is-worth-being-bad-at-activity-7468262826755653632-GOrG",
      "shareLinkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:share:7468262824067145728",
      "shareUrn": "urn:li:share:7468262824067145728",
      "content": "Anything worth doing is worth being bad at first.\n\nThat's my personal philosophy for learning anything. And it's exactly how I teach AI.\n\nWhen we go into companies to train teams, one of the first things I tell leaders is: do not care how good your team's first AI playbooks, agents, or prompts are.\n\nThat is not the point.\n\nIn fact, if you push your team to start with the highest-value use cases, you've just added performance pressure to what should be a learning environment.\n\nThe result? It takes so much longer to actually get good.\n\nThe teams that win with AI aren't the ones who demanded excellence from day one. They're the ones who gave their people room to experiment, fail, and iterate on low-stakes work first.\n\nThis is true whether you're leading a team or learning on your own.\n\nThe people who look effortless at this right now? They've been at it for years. And they were bad at it at first too.\n\nSo if you're a leader watching your team's early AI output and feeling anxious about the quality: that's the wrong metric. The right metric is, are they building the habit? Are they getting reps in?",
      "author": {
        "id": "ACoAAA-GcZEB1Efo6vRJ-H8ZWRM-70gtvWadWGQ",
